.page linux 1 run a program built for Linux

# SYNOPSIS
*linux* [*-r*] _PROGRAM_ [_ARGUMENT_...]

*native* _PROGRAM_ [_ARGUMENT_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`linux` starts _PROGRAM_ under the Linux personality of the kernel: the program makes its system calls with the numbers and structures of x86-64 Linux instead of the ones native TouhouOS programs use, and it starts with the stack a Linux program expects (arguments, environment, auxiliary vector). _PROGRAM_ is a path (nothing is searched for through `PATH`). It can be a static executable, a position-independent one, a dynamically linked one (the kernel loads its dynamic linker too) or a `#!` script.

When a Linux root is mounted on `/linux` (see linuxroot(8)), the program lives in that tree: _PROGRAM_, its dynamic linker, its libraries and every file it opens are looked up under `/linux`, so `linux /bin/ls /` lists the root of the distribution that was unpacked there. Only `/proc`, `/dev`, `/tmp`, `/sys` and `/run` are the machine's own. Without a Linux root every path is a real one.

The personality is inherited by `fork` and kept by `execve`: a Linux program that starts another program makes that one a Linux program too. Threads, signals (the real-time signals with their `siginfo` included), interval and POSIX timers, sockets (IPv4 and UNIX-domain, with descriptors in messages), `poll`, `epoll`, `eventfd` and `timerfd` are implemented. A system call the personality does not implement fails with `ENOSYS` and is named once in the kernel log (`dmesg`, tag `Outside World`).

Add `linux.trace` to the kernel command line to log every Linux system call with its result.

`native` starts _PROGRAM_ (searched for through `PATH`) under the native personality again. It is meant for a native tool started from inside a Linux program; that does not work yet, because a program started by a Linux process keeps the Linux personality.

# OPTIONS
@ *-r*
    Run _PROGRAM_ in the real root even when a Linux root is mounted. The static test programs in `/ScarletDevilMansion/share/linux-tests` live there.

# EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the program. 2 for a missing operand, 126 if the program could not be run, 127 if it was not found.

# EXAMPLES
    linux -r /ScarletDevilMansion/share/linux-tests/lnx_hello
    linux -r ./hello-static a b c
    linux /bin/ls /
    linux /bin/sh -c 'echo $((1+2))'

# NOTES
Not there yet: `signalfd`, IPv6 sockets and a vDSO. The files under `/proc` have this system's format and not Linux's, so `ps` and `top` of a Linux distribution do not work. A file mapping is copied when it is made (there is no page cache), so a program that maps many libraries starts a little slowly.

# SEE ALSO
linuxroot(8), dmesg(1), gensokyo(7)
